uirements.yaml, but I don't have that file available in my context, so I'll need to work with what I can access.\n\nSince the constraint specifies that every requirement has a default weight of 1 unless otherwise specified, I'll proceed with that assumption. Now I'm mapping out the requirements for release 06.0 (mktemp, ln, unlink) which are all marked as ready in requirement_states, starting with the mktemp requirements across its requirement groups. Looking at the constraint that each task can have at most 4 sub-requirements with a maximum weight of 4, I need to figure out how to distribute these requirements across my 4 available tasks strategically. Since the default weight is 1 per requirement, I'm essentially limited to 4 sub-requirements per task. Let me evaluate which requirements would have the most impact given these constraints.\n\nThe earliest incomplete release is 06.0, which needs three utilities implemented: mktemp, ln, and unlink. Unlink is the simplest since it just needs to call the unlink system call on a single file, though it does have 10 requirements to satisfy. I'm wondering whether I actually need to propose a contract task for these cmd/ packages at all, since they're command-line utilities rather than library packages—the contract is essentially the main.go file itself, not exported types like you'd have with pkg/ packages.\n\nRe-reading the constraint about not proposing implementation tasks for packages without contracts, I think that's really aimed at library packages that should have formal contracts. Since these cmd/ packages don't exist yet and don't export anything, I can probably skip the contract step and go straight to implementation tasks. I'll focus on proposing 4 tasks maximum, starting with unlink since it's the simplest, then move on to mktemp or ln depending on how many requirements I can fit into the remaining task slots. I'm realizing I need to split the unlink requirements much more granularly—with 10 requirements total and a 4-requirement limit per task, I can fit the implementation across 3 tasks, but then I still need separate tasks for tests, which violates the constraint that tests must be split from implementation.\n\nLooking at the actual requirements more carefully, the unlink utility is quite simple—the first three requirements are probably just basic file unlinking, the next four handle error cases and edge conditions, and the last three cover help/version flags and general options. Since I don't have explicit weight data, I'm assuming each requirement has a default weight of 1, which means I genuinely need multiple tasks just to stay under the 4-requirement ceiling.\n\nGiven the constraint to focus on the earliest incomplete release, I should prioritize Release 06.0 which has mktemp, ln, and unlink all marked as ready. Let me sketch out a task breakdown that mixes these simpler utilities rather than dwelling on unlink alone.\n\nBut I'm running into a structural problem: the guidelines say each SRD requirement maps to exactly one task, yet tests need to reference the same requirements as their corresponding implementation. So I can split unlink across multiple tasks for implementation, then create separate test tasks that reference those same requirements without violating the constraint.\n\nLooking at the requirement structure, I see unlink has three groups of requirements—R1.x, R2.x, and R3.x—which suggests a natural way to organize the work. Let me map out how to distribute these across tasks while respecting both the line budget and the requirement-to-task mapping rule. I'm realizing the constraint of 4 SRD requirements per task is too restrictive for a simple utility like unlink—splitting its 30-line implementation across 3 tasks would result in tiny, meaningless chunks. The intent is probably to group multiple small utilities together to hit the 250-350 line target per task, not to artificially fragment a single tool. I'm re-reading the constraints more carefully—each task can have at most 4 SRD sub-requirements with a total weight not exceeding 4. So with default weight 1, that's a maximum of 4 R-items per task, though higher weights would reduce that number. Given that unlink alone has 10 requirements, I'd need at least 3 tasks just for implementation plus another for tests, which already exceeds my 4-task budget. I think the best strategy is to start with the simplest requirement from the earliest ready release and work from there, accepting that I can't cover everything comprehensively.\n\nI'm realizing I don't have access to the requirements.yaml file to check actual weights, so I'll have to assume the default weight of 1 for now. I also need to consider whether cmd/ packages require a contract task—since they're in package main with no exported types, the contract requirement might not apply the same way it does for library packages.\n\nLet me focus on proposing four concrete tasks for the unlink command, which has the fewest requirements to implement.\n\nActually, I'm realizing that splitting the implementation across three separate tasks creates artificial dependencies—task 1 depends on task 0, task 2 depends on task 1, and task 3 depends on task 2, all because they're modifying the same file. Breaking a 30-50 line program into three implementation chunks doesn't feel right.\n\nI should reconsider whether I'm interpreting the constraint too strictly. Maybe some of these R-items are really just different aspects of a single requirement, not truly independent sub-requirements that warrant separate tasks.\n\nBut re-reading the constraint: \"Each SRD requirement maps to exactly one task requirement. Do not inflate counts by splitting a single requirement into sub-bullets, and do not deflate counts by merging unrelated requirements.\" That's pretty clear—I need a 1:1 mapping.\n\nThere's also a tension between P9, which says code tasks should target 5-8 requirements, and the constraints section, which says at most 4 SRD sub-requirements per task. The constraints section is more specific, so it should take precedence.\n\nI think the distinction is that task requirements in the description map 1:1 to SRD sub-requirements, and I'm capped at 4 per task. P9's guidance is probably aspirational for larger tasks that haven't hit the weight limit. So I'll stick with the max of 4 SRD sub-requirements.\n\nLet me propose 4 well-structured tasks for unlink since it's the simplest utility available in the earliest release. Actually, I should check if any SRD requirements have weight greater than 1. Looking at srd070-fmt's structure—where most groups have a single sub-requirement except group 13 which has four—it seems the SRD has been reorganized so that weight-4 requirements are split into their own groups.\n\nThe roadmap confirms this: all SRDs have been restructured so each weight-4 requirement gets its own group. This means weight-4 requirements do exist and are isolated, though without seeing the actual weights in srd038-unlink, I'll assume most are weight 1 by default. Now I need to decide whether to combine unlink's implementation into fewer tasks or keep them separate. The constraint is clear—each task can contain at most 4 SRD sub-requirements—so I should stick with my original plan rather than trying to merge related R-items just because they're in the same group.\n\nLet me organize the tasks meaningfully: Task 0 covers the core unlink operation and basic error handling with R1.1 through R2.1, Task 1 handles the remaining error cases plus help and version flags with R2.2 through R3.1, and Task 2 wraps up the final options with R3.2 and R3.3. That leaves Task 3 for the test suite.\n\nNow I'm realizing the constraint applies to test tasks too — each requirement maps to exactly one task, and test tasks can reference the same requirements as implementation tasks. But if I'm limited to 4 requirements per task, I'd need multiple test tasks as well, which would push me well over the 4-task budget. I think the 4-requirement limit still applies to test tasks, so I'd need to split them across at least two test tasks to cover all the same requirements.\n\nThe absurdity here is that I'm trying to apply this complex task-splitting structure to what's essentially a simple utility that installs a signal handler and calls the unlink syscall. I'm wondering if there's a more practical approach to this.\n\nI think the key insight is that I should combine the implementation into tasks with at most 4 SRD sub-requirements each, and then consolidate all the tests into a single test task. Since the differential test pattern is the same across all requirements and the test file uses a single TestDiff function with a slice of test cases, it naturally covers everything in one go. The acceptance criteria would just be that all differential tests pass, rather than trying to enumerate every single SRD requirement in the task breakdown.\n\nLooking back at the planning constitution example, it seems like task requirements don't have to map 1:1 with SRD sub-requirements—the example shows 3 task requirements covering multiple SRD items conceptually. The constraint is that each task can contain at most 4 SRD sub-requirements, and I should split any task that exceeds that limit. So a task requirement is different from an SRD sub-requirement, and I need to be careful about that distinction.\n\nGiven the 4-task limit and focusing on earliest releases, I'm proposing splitting the cmd/unlink implementation across multiple tasks: the first covering core functionality with R1.1 through R2.1, the second handling error handling and SIGPIPE with R2.2 through R3.1, and a third for remaining requirements.\n\nFor the test task, I realize I should define test-specific requirements rather than re-listing implementation ones—so I'd have four task requirements covering the differential test harness, core unlink operation coverage, error case coverage, and option handling coverage respectively.\n\nBut now I'm hitting a constraint issue: P6 says each SRD requirement maps to exactly one task, and I can have at most 4 SRD sub-requirements per task. If I'm testing 10+ sub-requirements across test tasks, I'd need to split them across multiple test tasks, which becomes impractical. I think the pragmatic approach is that when tasks are split into implementation and test, both reference the same SRD requirements—so if the implementation task covers R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, and R2.1, the test task covers those same requirements. But with the 4-task limit, I can only fit so much.\n\nGiven the constraints, I'm realizing that for cmd/ packages, the differential test might actually serve as the implementation verification itself, and the test file could be small enough to combine with the last implementation task. But P2 explicitly forbids a single task from both implementing and testing the same feature, so that's not an option. Let me step back and think about this differently—maybe I should focus on covering different utilities to maximize what I can actually accomplish within the limits.\n\nActually, I'm wondering if some requirements might have weights greater than one. If a requirement carries weight 4, it would get its own task. Looking at srd038-unlink specifically, the groups are R1 through R3, each with multiple sub-requirements, so none of them appear to be weight-4 groups—they're likely all weight-1. That means I need to split the work across 4 tasks: the first three tasks handle implementation across the requirements, and the fourth covers differential testing for the initial batch.\n\nBut wait, tasks 0 through 2 all modify the same file, which is fine since they have dependencies. For the test task, I'll reference the first four requirements since the test covers them, with future test tasks handling the rest. Actually, I'm realizing that the test for a cmd/ package is itself a differential test—the test file has one TestDiff function with multiple test cases that all run together, so I can't really split tests by individual requirement. The test task is really about writing the differential test harness, which implicitly covers all requirements. Let me restructure this so the test task requirements describe the test structure itself rather than mapping one-to-one to SRD requirements: building and discovering the reference binary, testing successful unlink operations, and handling error cases.\n\nThat said, the constraint requires each SRD requirement to map to exactly one task requirement, so I should probably list the SRD sub-requirements in the task title. Though I'm wondering if I'm overcomplicating this—maybe I should pick a utility with fewer requirements to start with. Looking at the data, whoami has only 7 requirements compared to unlink's 10, so that might be a better choice.\n\nActually, I'm realizing that even the smallest utilities have 7 or more requirements, and with a maximum of 4 per task and 4 tasks total, I can only fully cover one utility with both implementation and tests. Let me focus on the earliest release with ready requirements—that's 06.0 with mktemp, ln, and unlink. I'll propose 4 tasks for unlink since it's the simplest of the three.\n\nBut wait, I need to reconsider whether cmd/ utilities require contracts. The constraint says I shouldn't propose implementation tasks for new packages without a contract task unless the package already exists. cmd/unlink is new and doesn't exist yet. For pkg/ packages, contracts define exports, but cmd/ packages are just main.go with a main function—a contract would essentially be an empty stub. I think the constraint's intent is really about pkg/ packages, though technically it applies to all new packages. If I follow it literally, I'd need to start with a contract task that creates the main.go skeleton, then move into implementation.\n\nThat approach wastes one of my four available tasks on a stub file, which isn't ideal. I'm going to interpret this pragmatically: cmd/