it seems like the task should reference which SRD requirements it covers in the title, not that each task requirement must be an SRD requirement. So for the test task, I could anchor it to srd038 overall and list the test coverage as the task requirements—things like creating the test function, building the binary, looking up the reference implementation, and ensuring all srd038 sub-requirements are tested.\n\nFor split tasks where both implementation and test reference the same requirement, that counts as one requirement mapping to two tasks. The 4-per-task limit still applies to each task individually, so a test task covering all 10 sub-requirements would exceed that limit. I need to either split the test task further or accept that it references multiple requirements.\n\nLet me just commit to a concrete plan: focus on cmd/unlink from release 06.0, split the work into implementation and test tasks, and anchor each to srd038 while keeping the task requirements within reasonable scope. I'm reconsidering whether spreading effort across all three utilities is worth it—starting ln and mktemp while leaving unlink half-done means nothing actually compiles or works. I think it's better to complete unlink fully, even if it means the other utilities don't get touched in this release.\n\nThe test situation is tricky too: writing differential tests means one test file with all cases, so splitting test tasks across multiple implementation tasks doesn't make sense—each subsequent task would just be extending the same file. So I'm planning to structure this as consecutive implementation tasks for unlink, each covering a chunk of requirements, rather than trying to parallelize across different utilities.\n\nWith 10 requirements total and a 4-requirement limit per task, I need at least 3 implementation tasks just for unlink. Adding a test task makes 4 tasks total for this utility. That leaves no room to start another utility in this batch, which feels inefficient, but trying to squeeze unlink into 2 tasks would leave 2 requirements uncovered.\n\nLet me see if I can redistribute the requirements more evenly. If I move R3.1 from task 1 to task 2, I'd have task 1 at 3 requirements and task 2 at 3 requirements, which gives me more flexibility and better balance across the tasks. Task 3 is the differential test file for cmd/unlink, covering the same requirements as Task 0 with a weight of 4, and it depends on Task 2 being complete. I realize I can't actually read the SRD to understand what those requirement codes mean, but the stitch agent will handle that when it processes the required_reading field in the task descriptions. I need to balance specificity with not overstepping—the stitch agent should read the SRD for exact details, but my task descriptions should be clear about what needs implementing. So I'll reference the SRD R-IDs while being concrete about the behavior: accept one filename, validate arguments, handle exit codes correctly, and report errors to stderr when files don't exist. I'm realizing I need to be careful not to guess what the SRD requirements actually contain. Rather than assuming what R1.1, R1.2, and so on specify, I should reference the SRD document directly and let the stitch agent read it to understand the exact requirements. I'll structure the task to reference the specific requirement IDs from srd038-unlink so there's no ambiguity about what needs to be implemented. I'm reconsidering the constraint about not exceeding 4 tasks and the priority to focus on the earliest incomplete release. Looking at the requirement states, the earliest release with ready requirements is 06.0 (mktemp, ln, unlink), but I'm noticing that several earlier releases have utilities not yet started—and I'm wondering whether these are being tracked elsewhere or if they should be included in this task list. The earliest actionable release is 06.0 since all its requirements are ready, so I'll focus on four tasks for cmd/unlink. For the stitch agent to follow existing patterns, I should point them to the SRD, similar command implementations like cmd/true and cmd/basename, the SIGPIPE handler in pkg/sys/, and the differential testing utilities in pkg/testutils/.\n\nLooking at what's actually implemented, cmd/basename is the best reference since it handles argument processing similarly to what unlink will need, unlike the simpler exit-only commands or those with different patterns like yes or tee.\n\nNow I'm realizing the real challenge: tasks 0-2 all modify the same file, so task 1's stitch agent needs to understand what task 0 created without being explicitly told.\n\nThe fix is straightforward—include cmd/unlink/main.go in the required_reading for tasks 1 and 2. That way, each agent can read the current state of the file and extend it appropriately, which aligns with the constraint that every task must be independently executable.\n\nBut now I'm realizing the real issue: splitting a 30-line utility across three implementation tasks creates more friction than it solves. Each agent will try to write a complete program, and the partial implementations will confuse subsequent agents. I could combine the implementation tasks into one, but that would exceed the 4 sub-requirement limit. Or I could reduce the test coverage, but that feels like cutting corners. I need to figure out what the right balance actually is here. that for very simple utilities, the 4-sub-req limit means the implementation task covers a subset, and subsequent tasks add more. But for unlink, \"a subset\" of the implementation doesn't make sense - it's either a complete program or it's not.\n\nUnless... task 0 creates a complete but minimal program that handles the core case (4 reqs), task 1 adds error handling (4 reqs), and task 2 adds remaining options (2 reqs). Each task produces a working program that progressively handles more cases.\n\nTask 0: Creates main.go that handles the basic case (take one arg, call unlink, exit 0/1)\nTask 1: Modifies main.go to add detailed error messages and edge cases\nTask 2: Modifies main.go to add --help/--version and SIGPIPE\n\nEach is a self-contained modification. The program works after each task, just handles fewer cases.\n\nThis approach makes sense—the program compiles and runs after task 0, then task 1 adds robustness with better error handling, and task 2 polishes it with command-line options and signal handling. So I'll structure it as three incremental modifications to the same file, each building on the previous work. I'm realizing the differential test pattern actually exercises the entire binary against the reference implementation, so the test coverage naturally extends beyond just the specific requirements I listed. The SRD references are really about traceability rather than constraining what gets tested. I should restructure the task requirements to reflect that the test function will comprehensively cover all implemented behavior across the full requirement range, with the build and discovery logic handled separately. I'm realizing the test task can reference the same SRD requirements as implementation tasks since they're split across different deliverables, but I still need to respect the 4 sub-requirement limit per task. Rather than listing all the requirements I want to cover, I should pick the most critical ones and be specific about which sub-requirements each test case addresses.\n\nThe key insight is that the stitch agent will naturally write comprehensive tests once it reads the SRD, so I don't need to artificially constrain it—the 4-requirement limit is just for task planning and traceability, not for limiting what the agent actually produces. I'll structure the task to list 4 specific sub-requirements for scope clarity, but the acceptance criteria can ask for comprehensive differential test coverage, letting the agent write all the necessary test cases.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the overall decomposition—instead of three implementation tasks plus one test task for unlink, maybe I should do two tasks per feature (implementation and tests) to keep things more balanced. But that runs into the constraint that each task can only list 4 sub-requirements, so I'd need to split the 8 unlink requirements across two tasks anyway. Let me think through whether this actually improves the structure or if my original plan is cleaner.\n\nThe depth-first approach—fully implementing unlink across three tasks and then testing it—seems more aligned with the priority to focus on the earliest incomplete release. A partially implemented utility won't pass any tests, so it makes more sense to complete one feature fully rather than spread effort across multiple utilities with none of them fully working.\n\nNow I'm writing out the YAML structure. I need to make sure the task titles include the requirement IDs as specified, something like \"cmd/unlink core (srd038 R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R2.1)\". And I should verify the weight calculations for each task before finalizing the output.\n\nFor the required_reading sections, I'll reference existing cmd implementations like basename, dirname, and true as patterns the developer can follow.\n\nI'm also considering the design decisions: whether to use syscall.Unlink() directly since that matches what GNU unlink does, versus os.Remove() which handles both files and directories. The unlink(2) system call is specifically for files, so syscall.Unlink() is the right choice here.\n\nNow I'm putting together the YAML structure for the task breakdown... I need to handle error output to stderr when the unlink operation fails, and I'm also setting up the design approach to follow the same patterns as the basename utility while using syscall.Unlink directly to match GNU behavior and reject directories. The acceptance criteria will verify the binary compiles and runs correctly with the proper exit codes and error handling.\n\nNow I'm looking at the next task which involves implementing additional error hand